WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
The first film turned recombinant offspring into a short chromosome ruler.

2
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.420
Why does that ruler fail when the interval grows?

3
00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:11.820
Reuse the blue and gold homologues.

4
00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:17.580
Move their outside markers apart, and let more crossover sparks fall between them.

5
00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:22.440
The map keeps growing, but the observed count cannot follow forever.

6
00:00:25.983 --> 00:00:28.983
A two-point cross does not count crossover events.

7
00:00:30.203 --> 00:00:33.623
It reads only the final combination of the outside markers.

8
00:00:34.903 --> 00:00:40.023
One exchange between them flips a parental combination into a recombinant one.

9
00:00:41.263 --> 00:00:44.183
Another exchange can flip that combination again.

10
00:00:45.423 --> 00:00:48.503
The gamete remembers its ending, not the number of turns.

11
00:00:49.633 --> 00:00:53.233
The chromatid now looks recombinant at the outside markers.

12
00:00:54.573 --> 00:00:59.633
What will those markers show after another crossover lands inside the same interval?

13
00:01:00.953 --> 00:01:04.073
Follow only their colours, not the crossed segments in the middle.

14
00:01:09.667 --> 00:01:13.447
The second flip can restore the parental outside combination.

15
00:01:14.727 --> 00:01:19.127
The middle still records exchanged material, but a two-point score misses it.

16
00:01:20.327 --> 00:01:22.687
Even numbers of effective flips look parental.

17
00:01:24.087 --> 00:01:25.587
Odd numbers look recombinant.

18
00:01:27.047 --> 00:01:31.607
Long intervals contain more hidden histories that finish with the same visible

19
00:01:31.607 --> 00:01:32.607
answer.

20
00:01:33.167 --> 00:01:38.547
Across many meioses, repeated flips make parental and recombinant endings equally

21
00:01:38.547 --> 00:01:43.867
likely. Recombination frequency therefore approaches its ceiling.

22
00:01:45.167 --> 00:01:49.627
So distant linked loci can look unlinked, like loci on different chromosomes.

23
00:01:51.027 --> 00:01:54.787
A two-point result at the ceiling cannot distinguish those cases.

24
00:01:55.917 --> 00:01:58.777
Now plot what the count can see against map distance.

25
00:02:00.057 --> 00:02:02.957
Use Haldane's no-interference equation, not a sketch.

26
00:02:04.217 --> 00:02:08.557
The computed curve starts close to a straight line, then bends toward the ceiling.

27
00:02:10.317 --> 00:02:15.417
Distance continues across the axis while the observable frequency loses sensitivity.

28
00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:20.400
A third marker breaks a long span into shorter intervals.

29
00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:24.940
Count each short interval where fewer exchanges stay hidden.

30
00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:29.080
Place those distances end to end on one chromosome.

31
00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:31.880
Their map distances add.

32
00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:38.280
Their raw recombination frequencies do not, because the whole-span count can erase

33
00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:39.380
double crossovers.

34
00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:44.210
The computed curve is a model, not a universal correction.

35
00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:48.370
Haldane assumes independent crossover placement.

36
00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:54.850
Interference changes how often nearby crossovers occur, so another function may fit

37
00:02:54.850 --> 00:03:01.250
better. The durable practice is to map short intervals, add their distances, and

38
00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:03.070
state the model behind any correction.

39
00:03:04.233 --> 00:03:06.693
The count reports a final marker state.

40
00:03:07.873 --> 00:03:12.453
The map represents the expected number of crossovers per chromatid across the

41
00:03:12.453 --> 00:03:18.053
interval. Those quantities track closely only while hidden exchanges are rare.

42
00:03:19.353 --> 00:03:23.613
As the interval grows, the map can keep lengthening after the count has nearly

43
00:03:23.613 --> 00:03:24.473
stopped changing.
